A43 — send the game window note on the first top-level map

The daemon clears the app's booting splash when it receives a GameWindowNote on
the scan-out channel. Only the guest ICD shim sends one, from its swapchain
present path, so a game that never presents through the shim's WSI proxy keeps
the splash up while it runs behind it: an OpenGL game whose zink output reaches
X as plain drawing (Balatro), a GDI game, or a static dialog such as an assert
MessageBox.

The note was first sent on a commit-rate gate: while no override surface was
bound, the focused wine desktop window's main-surface commits were counted, and
a second with at least 20 of them and a game child mapped sent it. A static
dialog never reaches that rate, so it stayed hidden behind the splash. The note
now follows the X11 server's rule, where the app hides the splash on the first
mapped window, whatever it is: the compositor sends it on the first map of a
top-level window (in practice wine's desktop window), on a fresh
GAMENATIVE_DRM_SOCKET connection.

The daemon serves only the newest scan-out connection, so a note can be dropped
when another client connects right after it. It is sent again on a later
commit, at most once a second and at most three times in all; the daemon
latches the first.

